# Which plastics float?
#
# Recycling sorts plastics by density, and you can do it in a glass. Every
# one of these is a solid, all of them look much the same, and the water
# tells them apart without weighing anything: what is lighter than the
# liquid comes to the top, and what is heavier goes to the bottom.
register lv2

# 1. Polypropylene, 0.90 g/mL — lighter than water.
add v1 water 200mL
add v1 PP 5g
measure v1 density
look v1

# 2. Polystyrene, 1.05 g/mL. Barely heavier — but heavier is heavier. Give
#    it a couple of minutes: a solid that has just been dropped in is still
#    swirling, and the bench says so before it says where it ended up.
new beaker
add v2 water 200mL
add v2 PS 5g
wait 2min
look v2

# 3. PET, 1.38 g/mL, the drinks-bottle plastic. Heavier again.
new beaker
add v3 water 200mL
add v3 PET 5g
wait 2min
look v3

# The sorting worked: one floats, two sink, and nothing was weighed. That
# is the whole of the industrial method — a float-sink tank with the liquid
# set to a chosen density.
#
# What this does not model: the density belongs to the MATERIAL, not to the
# piece. A hollow bottle of PET floats in a real sink and this bench would
# still call PET heavier than water, because it has no shape to reason
# about — the same boundary the density meter states. Surface tension,
# which really does hold small light objects up, is not modelled either.
#
# And a caution the bench prints for itself: raising the water's density
# with salt is how you would separate the two sinkers, and this model
# cannot do it. Every ion in the registry carries water's density as a
# placeholder, so salt water reads exactly 1.00 g/mL. The meter says so
# rather than letting you believe the brine.
new beaker
add v4 water 200mL
add v4 NaCl 60g
measure v4 density
